  await when(() => spy.mock.calls.length > 0);
  //    (the store's autorun under test must be the one calling `spy`,
  //     e.g. it invokes a spy-able injected dependency)

  // 2. Baseline, so a later increment is unambiguous.
  const callsBeforeDispose = spy.mock.calls.length;

  store.dispose();

  // 3. Trigger the very mutation that fired it the first time.
  store.setSomething(2);

  // 4. Give the reaction a fair chance to run if it were still live —
  //    a microtask/tick, not a fixed sleep. A still-live autorun would
  //    schedule and flush here.
  await Promise.resolve();

  // 5. The assertion that actually proves disposal.
  expect(spy.mock.calls.length).toBe(callsBeforeDispose);
});
```

## Key points about the shape

- **Establish liveness first.** Trigger the tracked observable before `dispose()` and wait (`await when(...)`) until the effect has provably run. Without this baseline, step 5 could pass simply because the autorun was never wired up correctly in the first place — which is a different bug.
- **Dispose, then re-trigger the same mutation.** Re-firing the identical dependency change is what distinguishes "stopped" from "happened to not fire this time."
- **Wait after the re-trigger, but not with a sleep.** A still-live autorun schedules its effect on the MobX reaction queue; a microtask flush (`await Promise.resolve()`, or an explicit `await when(() => false)`-style drain if the store uses async reactions) gives it the chance to run. Do **not** use `setTimeout(resolve, 100)` — it is slower than necessary and still not deterministic under CI load. If the effect is genuinely asynchronous inside, wait on a `when(...)` predicate over the spy instead.
- **If the autorun's side effect isn't directly observable**, inject a spy-able dependency (e.g. a mock API/logger the autorun calls) at store construction so the effect is assertable. Constructing the store with mocked dependencies is the right setup either way, per `rules/testing.mdc`.

